Each country has its unique context for its development, which varies to some extent. It's not always an imported idea or something imposed from outside its context. Perhaps the political geography and boundaries of countries and ruling systems change, expanding or contracting, rising or falling, due to external or internal factors, or military coups. However, these states were not created by others out of nothing, and they might have developed independently from the Sykes-Picot Agreement entirely. For example, the third Saudi state began in 1902 and emerged independently, just like the Sultanate of Oman did before it, for many years. The monarchy in Iraq was overthrown by the brutal coup of Abdul Karim Qassem in 1958, and the United Arab Emirates (UAE) was established through understanding, cooperation, and awareness of the union's importance. There is no role for Sykes-Picot in either the UAE or any other country, and their state was declared in 1971. Many other examples exist where British or French protection in North Africa predated the Sykes-Picot Agreement by several decades.
